<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>5.12.3: Graphs Representing Exponential Decay</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m01465</md:content-id>
    <md:title>5.12.3: Graphs Representing Exponential Decay</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d71f5909-46f8-411d-866b-a01e4393d45e</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
    <section id="sect-00001">
      <title>Activity</title>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00001">
        <item>Desmos activity: Exponential Graph Maps</item>
      </list>
      <note class="link-to-learning" id="note-00001">
        <para id="para-00001">Log into <link url="https://student.desmos.com">student.desmos.com</link> using the information provided by your teacher to complete the activity.</para>
      </note>
      <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00002">
        <item>Functions <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math><emphasis> </emphasis>are defined by these equations:</item>
      </list>
      <list id="list-00003">
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>000</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
        <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>000</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
      </list>
        <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00004">
          <item>Use the graphing tool or technology outside the course. On the same coordinate plane, sketch a graph that represents the functions, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> and <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>. It may be helpful to use a different color for each function.</item>
      </list><media alt="">
      <iframe src="../../media/desmos/56a7de02b0c3c9e3efa76644bf1d8715b0ceb097.html" width="500" height="400"/></media>
        <exercise id="exer-00001">
          <problem id="prob-00001">
            <para id="para-00002">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00001">
            <para id="para-00003">Compare your answers:</para>
            <para id="para-00004">
                      <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_06_01.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <!-- BEGIN ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->

        <exercise id="_2d4da33c-831a-4cfe-8c72-6986754a18f6" class="multiple-choice">
          <problem id="prob-00002">
            <para id="para-00005">Which function is decaying more quickly?</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00002">
            <para id="para-00006">Correct! <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
            <para id="para-00007">The correct answer is: <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math></para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>

      <!--END ACTIVITY DROP DOWN 1 TRY -->
      
        <exercise id="exer-00002">
          <problem id="prob-00003">
            <para id="para-00008">Explain your reasoning regarding which function decays more quickly.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00003">
            <para id="para-00009">Compare your answers:</para>
            <para id="para-00010"><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>000</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> has a smaller base than <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>g</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>000</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>9</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. Both bases are less than 1, so both functions decrease, but <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>000</m:mn><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mfrac><m:mrow><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow><m:mrow><m:mn>10</m:mn></m:mrow></m:mfrac><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math> decreases more rapidly.
</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      
      <note id="note-00002"><title>Building Character: Grit</title>
              <media alt="Insert Alt Text">
              <image mime-type="image/png" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_06_02.png"/>
            </media>
          
          
            <para id="para-00011">Grit predicts accomplishing challenging goals of personal significance. For example, grittier students are more likely to graduate from high school, and grittier cadets are more likely to complete their training at West Point.</para>
            <para id="para-00012">Think about your current sense of grit. Are the following statements true for you?</para>
            <list id="list-00005">
              <item>
          There is at least one subject or activity that I never get bored of thinking about.
       </item>
              <item>
          I finish whatever I begin.
       </item>
            </list>
            <para id="para-00013">Don’t worry if none of these statements are true for you. Developing this trait takes time. Your first step starts today!</para>
          
        
      </note>

    </section>
    <note class="self-check" id="note-00003">
      <para id="para-00014">Which of the following functions will decay the fastest?</para>
    </note>
    <section id="sect-00003">
      <title>Additional Resources</title>
      
    <section id="sect-00004">
      <title>Matching Exponential Functions and Graphs</title>
      <para id="para-00015">For each of the following, match each function with one of the graphs in the picture below.</para>
      <para id="para-00016">
        
          <media alt="Graph of six exponential functions.">
            <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_08_01.jpg"/>
          </media>
        
    </para>
      <para id="para-00017">Remember, the equation for an exponential function is <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>. The <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> will affect the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept. When <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>&lt;</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, there is exponential decay. The closer <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is to 0, the faster it decays. The closer <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> is to 1, the slower it decays. When <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mo>&gt;</m:mo><m:mn>1</m:mn></m:mrow></m:math>, the larger the value of <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>b</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>, the faster it grows.</para>
      <table class="os-raise-wideadjustedtable" id="table-00001">
        <tgroup cols="4"><tbody><row>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>Equation</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis><m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>Growth/Decay</emphasis>
            </entry>
            <entry>
             <emphasis>Match</emphasis>
            </entry>
          </row>
        <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00006">
                <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.68</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry>
    <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>
    </entry>
            <entry>
     Decay
    </entry>
            <entry>
     B
    </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00007">
                <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1.28</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry>
     <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>
    </entry>
            <entry>
      Growth
    </entry>
            <entry>
      F
    </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00008">
                <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.81</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry>
    <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>
    </entry>
            <entry>
    Decay
    </entry>
            <entry>
    A
    </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00009">
                <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1.28</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry>
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>
    </entry>
            <entry>
      Growth
    </entry>
            <entry>
    D
    </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00010">
                <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>1.59</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry>
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>2</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>
    </entry>
            <entry>
     Growth
    </entry>
            <entry>
    E
    </entry>
          </row>
          <row>
            <entry>
              <list class="os-raise-noindent" list-type="enumerated" id="list-00011">
                <item><m:math><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0.68</m:mn><m:msup><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math></item>
              </list>
            </entry>
            <entry>
      <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mn>0</m:mn><m:mo>,</m:mo><m:mn>4</m:mn><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo></m:mrow></m:math>
    </entry>
            <entry>
      Decay
    </entry>
            <entry>
      C
    </entry>
          </row>
        </tbody></tgroup></table>
        <note class="try" id="note-00004">
      <title> Try It: Matching Exponential Functions and Graphs</title>
        <exercise id="exer-00003">
          <problem id="prob-00004">
            <para id="para-00018">
              
                <media alt="Graph of six exponential functions.">
                  <image mime-type="image/jpeg" src="../../media/Algebra-1_05_14_08_02.jpg"/>
                </media>
              
          </para>
            <para id="para-00019">In the graph above, which function, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, has the largest value for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>?</para>
            <para id="para-00020">Write down your answer, then select the <emphasis>solution</emphasis> button to compare your work.</para>
          </problem>
          <solution id="sol-00004">
            <para id="para-00021">Compare your answer:</para>
            <para id="para-00022">Here is how to identify which has the largest value for <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>:</para>
            <para id="para-00023">In the function <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>f</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">(</m:mo><m:mi>x</m:mi><m:mo stretchy="false">)</m:mo><m:mo>=</m:mo><m:mi>a</m:mi><m:mi>·</m:mi><m:msup><m:mi>b</m:mi><m:mi>x</m:mi></m:msup></m:mrow></m:math>, <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>a</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math> changes the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept. The function with the largest <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-intercept is C. It crosses the <m:math display="inline"><m:mrow><m:mi>y</m:mi></m:mrow></m:math>-axis at the highest point.</para>
          </solution>
        </exercise>
      </note>
    </section>
    </section>
    
  </content>
